The code statements that lead to the bug are:
module_param_named(def_reserved_size, def_reserved_size, int,
		   S_IRUGO | S_IWUSR);
Reason:
This registration lacks validation, allowing a user to write an invalid value (e.g., -1 or >1,048,576) via `/sys/module/sg/parameters/def_reserved_size`. When `sg_open` later calls `sg_build_reserve` with this unchecked size, it attempts to allocate a huge buffer, causing a soft lockup.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
static int def_reserved_size_set(const char *val, const struct kernel_param *kp)
{
	int size, ret;
	if (!val)
		return -EINVAL;
	ret = kstrtoint(val, 0, &size);
	if (ret)
		return ret;
	if (size < 0 || size > 1048576)
		return -ERANGE;
	def_reserved_size = size;
	return 0;
}
module_param_cb(def_reserved_size, &def_reserved_size_ops, &def_reserved_size,
		   S_IRUGO | S_IWUSR);
Reason:
A custom set function is introduced that validates the value (0 to 1 MB) before assigning it, and `module_param_cb` enforces this validation for all writes to the parameter, preventing the soft lockup.

